Not really.  A VAXstation II has a CPU board, memory, DEQNA, tube, 
keyboard, and mouse.  It's certainly cheaper to buy X-terms than to 
keep them on DEC maintenance, but the simplicity of debugging such
systems and the speed with which 3rd party maintenance people are willing
to ship new boards (Continental Computer, for example, has been both
speedy, reasonably priced, and rational to deal with in a University
environment) means that our maintenance costs for 3 systems have been
less than $500 total each year.  To replace them with X-terms would be
a minimum of $1200 a piece (19" tube, etc.), and the maintenance on
the X terms is non-zero.  Plus, let's include my time in evaluating
10 or 15 X-term vendors to find out which one is actually reasonable, plus the
University bidding process, etc.  No, I think for small numbers (i.e.,
1 to 10 systems), it will pay off for the next 3-4 years.  

