   My saga continues.  (Summary, I'm investigating removal of our HSC
and associated peripherals, and replacing them with direct attached gear.
My options seem to be DSSI and SCSI.  My system is VAX 6000 model 430.
This saga thread deals with disk; the HSC currently has 7.5 gigs in 8
spindles on 2 HSC disk channels.)

   I have received a suggestion that SCSI disks do not perform well if
the ports are (nearly) fully populated.  I have two vendors who say their
product does not exhibit this behaviour (my contacts are salesmen, after
all :-).  It is possible I may have 10 or more one-gig spindles flying off
2 BI-SCSI adapters (nearly fully populated as I measure it).  I may also
end up with a 9-track device on one of them, and perhaps even several
cartridge devices as well.

   If you are a biggish user of this approach (ie you are filling up your
SCSI ports) I'd like to hear from you.  More specifically, if you can offer
insight to either CMD's CBI-1000/M or CBI-1000/TM, or Clearpoint's DCMS-
D01 or D02 products, please do so.  Does the product perform in a reasonably
linear manner as additional spindles are added?

   DSSI seems immune to the performance issue, but appears to be more costly.
If you are running DSSDI, again with many ports in use, I'd like to hear your
experiences with this product as well.  Thanks.  b.
