In article <1991Oct28.221525.12562@cass.ma02.bull.com>, nstern@cass.ma02.bull.com (Neil Stern) writes...
>Does the VT320 terminal support its own emulator, or functionality
>as a super-set of the VT220 terminal or is it just a newer VT220.
>In other words, is there such a thing as an application that 
>requires vt320 emulation or support that won't work with a VT220
>emulator or terminal?

You've asked several questions. 1) The VT320 is not an emulator--it is the real
thing. :-)  [Sorry, couldn't help it.] 2) The VT320 is a functional superset of
the VT220. 3) There are basically three new features in the VT320: a status
display, support for the ISO Latin-1 (ISO 8859-1) character set, and terminal
state reports. [3a) The device attribute responses (device id) is different by
default but the primary ID can be modified in Set-Up.] 4) Yes, it is possible
to write an application for a VT320 that would not work the same on a VT220,
but only if you depend on the new features.

Tim Lasko, Digital Equipment Corp., Westford MA  (lasko@regent.enet.dec.com)
Disclaimer: My opinions are my own; the facts can speak for themselves.


