	Lars,

	  Technically the only machines we have that carry the DECsystem
	logo (as opposed to the DECstation logo) are:

		DECsystem 3100				January,1989
		DECsystem 5000/200			April, 1990
		DECsystem 5100				July, 1990
		DECsystem 5400				July, 1989
		DECsystem 5500				July, 1990
		DECsystem 5800 (5810 thru 5840)		July, 1989

	I believe that both the DECsystem 3100 and the DECsystem 5400 have
	been retired in favor of the DECsystem 5100 and the DECsystem 5500
	respectively. There aren't currently DECsystem 5000/1xx part numbers.
	Not that you can't very easily make a nice server out of them (and
	they make really nice department level machines, for small groups).

	I've added the rough introduction dates (as I remember them) next to 
	the machines. I'm not sure what you would term "old" (is two years 
	old?).

	Machine response time is pretty application and user quantity bound.

	The DS5000/200 series has the highest memory capacity and with the
	use of IPI disk drives over TURBOchannel you can get really sharp
	data performance. The DS5000/200 also would allow you to configure
	up to four SCSI buses which gives you up to 28 storage devices. As
	with any of the TURBOchannel based machines you can pick up third
	party options for multi-line asynch, centronics parallel printer
	interfaces and a host of other items that would help you configure
	a general purpose machine.

	Both the DS5100 and the DS5500 provide a Prestoserve option that
	can really boost your SCSI disk performance. This can be very
	helpful when you're dealing with a volume of users all writing 
	small records out to the SCSI drives at the same time.

	Most of the DS5800's that I've seen are in large number crunching
	applications. 

	Benchmark numbers and other performance numbers are available from
	your local DEC sales person. As for new machines...well there are
	always new machines in the pipeline. But you'll need to talk to
	your DEC rep about those too.

	The questions you've asked really need a detailed reply based on
	the environment you want to support (applications, users, instrumen-
	tation used, etc.) and the local DEC folks in Denmark should be able 
	to help with that.



