   This is configuration/performance question.  Our site is considering
a configuration change, part of which might involve backups over a network
to an IBM system.  Our system is a VAX 6000 model 430.  Part of the upgrade
involves increasing our 7.5 gigs of disk to about 10 gigs.
   The existing system has 2 DWMBA cards and 2 BI channels populated thus:

SYSGEN>  SHO/BUS
 Cpu Type: VAX 6000-430                        Cpu Connection: XMI
 Bus     Node    Generic Name              Nexus(hex)  Connection Address
 XMI 00   01    XMI - 6000-400 processor       0010
 XMI 00   02    XMI - 6000-400 processor       0020
 XMI 00   03    XMI - 6000-400 processor       0030
 XMI 00   09    XMI - memory module            0090
 XMI 00   0A    XMI - memory module            00A0
 XMI 00   0D    XMI - BI Adapter (DWMBA/A)         Connects to BI 00 node 01
   BI 00    01    BI - XMI Adapter (DWMBA/B)     00D1 Connects to XMI 00 node 0D
   BI 00    05    BI Combo Board (DMB32)         00D5
   BI 00    06    BI - NI Adapter (DEBNI)        00D6
 XMI 00   0E    XMI - BI Adapter (DWMBA/A)         Connects to BI 01 node 01
   BI 01    01    BI - XMI Adapter (DWMBA/B)     00E1 Connects to XMI 00 node 0E
   BI 01    04    CI                             00E4
   BI 01    06    BI - TK50 Adapter (TBK50)      00E6

   The configuration I'm investigating involves removal of the CI cards (and
attendant star coupler and HSC gear), and installing DSSI (dizzy? :-) disks
via Digital's KFMSA (XMI) interface.  Tape support would be reduced to one
9-track device probably via a KLESI-BB.  As a result, all the BI gear would
fit into one BI, allowing the removal of one BI channel, one DWMBA, the CIBCB
cards, all the HSC equipment, and result in the following configuration:

   Only BI - node 1	XMI adapter
                  2	TK50
                  3	KLESI-BB
                  4	-- open --
                  5	DMB-32
                  6	DEBNI

   My performance concerns:
   1)	Will backups over ethernet interfere significantly with other
	ethernet traffic?
   2)	If so, can/should I install a second DEBNI?
   3)	If so (again), How should the boards be configured to the "outside
	world"?  (a) Is there any danger of saturating the bandwidth of the
	only BI?  (b) Should I retain the second BI for any other reason,
	performance or other?  (c) Should the second DEBNI be dedicated to
	the connection to the IBM host, or can/should both DEBNI's share
	both loads?  (The IBM host becomes a peer DECnet node.)
   4)	Are there any complications I should be aware of wrt TGV Multinet?
   5)	Anything else I'm forgotten?

   For the curious, the following is from an earlier posting to comp.os.vms
(for which no one has provided any real response  )-:

