In article <1991Oct10.095639.2344@cl.cam.ac.uk> maj@ashby.cl.cam.ac.uk (Martyn Johnson) writes:


>> Can anybody enlighten me on why the SCSI connector on the back of
>> a DECstation 5000/120 is the opposite gender to that on a DECstation 3100?

>> Martyn Johnson      maj@cl.cam.ac.uk
>> University of Cambridge Computer Lab
>> Cambridge UK


	Martyn,

	  It's actually a little more than just gender. The pin count of
	the connector is different as well.


	* The VS3100 product was a combined SCSI/ST506 system. The connector
	  on that system was a Honda High-density 69 pin connector (the CHAMP
	  connector on the other end was still a 50 pin CHAMP).

	* The DS3100 design started after the VS3100 and it was dictated that
	  the box and cabling be common between the two (efficiency of cost).

	* With the design of the DS5000/xxx series the workstation group here
	  in Palo Alto determined to go with an industry standard SCSI cable
	  which is the 50 pin Honda High-density connector that you see.


	So, that's the history. I hope the explanation helps.

				Thanx,
				  AJ


