In article <1991Oct13.121106.1840@bsu-ucs.uucp> 00bestrawser@bsu-ucs.uucp (Bryan Strawser) writes:


>> I've had offers to buy both a VaxStation 2000 and a VaxStation II.  As far
>> as availble money goes, I've not got much.  So I need to get the most for my
>> buck.  

>> Could someone send me some details on the VaxStation II.  I can't find any
>> information in my Dec literature. What I need to know is how slow DecWindows
>> would be on it.  How many VUP's ?  What's the maximum number of users that
>> could be working atonce?  

>> Any other valuable information would be greatly appreciated.

>> Please e-mail!

>> Bryan



	Bryan,

	  It's been a long time since I worked on those systems, but here
	goes (to the best of my recollection):

		VS2000 (aka VAXstar)		VS II (aka Caylith)
		======				=====

VUP's		 .9				 .9


	They both use the MicroVAX II chip set. Though of different vintages.


Users		  2				  2


	That's the standard 2 User VMS license. Although, in this early a
	version of VAXstation, there was no firmware inhibition to prohibit
	more users (unlike the VS3500 series).



DECwindows

	That's going to be more a question of the memory configuration. The
	original systems were initially sold with as little as 2MB of memory.
	I don't believe that DECwindows is supportable (other than as an
	X-terminal device) on these machines in under 6MB (but I could be 
	wrong...some people will put up with really long response times).

	In terms of age, the VS II is an older vintange than the VS2000, so
	the chances of spares are probably better with the VS2000. But, the
	VS II system (frequently in a BA23 enclosure...that's the narrow
	desk side pedestal/ or in a BA123 enclosure...that's the wide desk
	side box on wheels) is far more configurable. It's also easier to
	do self maintenance on because you've got a Q-bus card cage and can
	swap out your own modules. The VS2000 is tougher to work on, it's
	12 screws to get into the box and then you're dealing with a very
	tight integration of parts.

	You might also find somebody who has GPX parts kicking around that
	you could upgrade your VS II with (you'd need the M7169 graphics
	driver and then either one or two M7168 modules depending upong how
	many planes you want...4 per I think...I'm actually amazed that I
	still remember the numbers...maybe I'm not as senile as Jim Gettys
	thinks I am). We used to sell this as a VCB02 upgrade I think. The
	GPX series also allowed a MUX configuration that gave you two heads
	(not Multi Screen Display like the DS5000/xxx, but Multi Head) as
	in each Monitor had its own keyboard and mouse.

	Storage devices also mount in the VS II and you can buy Q-bus SCSI
	adapters to get to the current capacity disks, while on the VS2000
	you're limited to an RD53 or RD54 type drive internally.

	If you buy the VS2000, make sure that you get one with an expansion
	pancake box on it (it's about 2 inches high and fixes onto the bottom
	of the VS2000 so that you can get additional disk drives).


	That's about all the memory dump I can manage. I hope that it helps.

				Thanx,
				  AJ


