In article <1991Oct11.151139.25580@newserve.cc.binghamton.edu> kym@bingvaxu.cc.binghamton.edu (R. Kym Horsell) writes:
>Speaking of which -- I understand the reason why the -15's used 18-bit
>memory (and the -10's later used 36) was something to do with lots
>of surplus byte-checked 16-bit core memory laying around at DEC. ;-)

Don't make up bogus folklore, even as a joke.

The PDP-15 came out *after* the PDP-10.  Perhaps you mean the PDP-7
and PDP-9?  But then again, the PDP-6 came out before those.  Yet
again, the PDP-4 came out before the PDP-6.

See the pattern?

Anyway, there was (and still is) nothing particularly magic to 8-bits
as a primary unit of storage.  It wasn't until IBM came out with the
S/360 (and the EBCDIC character set) c.1964 that people started
thinking in terms of bytes.  IBM probably would not have used either
bytes or variable length words if memory of the time hadn't been so
expensive that every bit counted.  The S/360 was (and its successors
remain) a dreadful architecture to program.

Prior to that time, all sorts of word sizes were used.  IBM was big on
4-bit BCD arithmetic.  5 and 6 bit character sets were common.

The PDP-1 (c.1959) was intended to be a small, low-cost computer (or
`programmed data processor') at a time when `computers' were monstrous
entities.  18-bit words gave it a fundamental unsigned integer of
magnitude up to 262,144.  It also stored three characters/word.  The
PDP-1 had a few models but no successors; future DEC 18-bit machines
were the successive PDP-4/7/9/15 family.

In the PDP-1 days, `core memory' was still a very new concept.  There
was *not* `lots of surplus byte-checked 16-bit core memory laying
around at DEC.'

The PDP-2 was to be a larger 24-bit computer that was never built.

The PDP-3 was to be a 36-bit computer; one was eventually built by a
customer.

The PDP-5 was a small 12-bit `minicomputer'.  It made perfect sense
considering the ideas behind the PDP-1 and PDP-4.  It was succeeded by
the famous PDP-8 family and the PDP-12.

The PDP-6 (c. 1964) was DEC's first `full sized' computer and nearly
bankrupted the company.  It was the first DEC machine designed for big
applications, especially Lisp which at the time was seen as something
very important.  It had a (huge for the time) 18-bit address space and
36-bit words.  The address space was thought to be much bigger than
anyone would ever actually have in terms of physical memory -- that
is, until MIT AI Lab bought the 256K Fabritek `moby' memory...  ;-)

Using the new 7-bit ASCII character set, it fit 5 characters/word with
a tag bit for over 1.25 million characters in an address space.  It
had fixed (72 bits) and floating point (9 bit exponent, 63 bit
mantissa) precision and magnitude still unrivalled in today's `modern'
computers.  Over and over again, I curse the S/360-brained cretins on
the ANSI C committee who gave us `double' and `long double' for
floating point but leave us with `long' as the best we can get for
fixed point.

The PDP-6 is, of course, the direct ancestor and largely compatible
