In article <1991Oct10.231319.1821@bsu-ucs.uucp>, 00bestrawser@bsu-ucs.uucp (Bryan Strawser) writes:
> I've been looking through the DEC literatur that I have.  I cannot
> find any reference to a VS 2100.  Could someone fill me in on it's
> capabilities.  A comarsion to a VS 3100/GPX would be helpful.

I don't think there is such a machine.  There's a DECstation 2100, but that
is RISC/ULTRIX, not VAX/VMS.  Are you sure you are not thinking of a VS2000?

> I'm just not sure how one would perform as a single user system running
> VMS 5.3 and DecWindows.  I would be using it for my own development
> projects.

The VAXstation 2000 has a MicroVAX II CPU (0.9 VUPS) but no bus.  Memory
and video boards are cabled directly to the motherboard.  For storage, it
uses RD-series drives which are slow and expensive.  You can (or could) get
a TK50 drive which had a modified SCSI interface.

As a development machine, you are likely to find the speed to be inadequate
for running DECwindows unless you have a lot of memory (max is 14Mb), and I
suspect that disk space might also be a problem unless you can cluster it
to another machine, or use NFS (over TCP/IP) to another system capable of
being an NFS server.

To sum up: yes, it could be useful, but you might be better off to look
around for a cheap VAXstation 3100.  The new VAXstations are due out Real
Soon Now, which will make the 3100s much cheaper.

