In article <12455@plains.NoDak.edu>, 
	enders@plains.NoDak.edu (Todd Enders - WD0BCI ) writes:

|>      With all the talk of multi-processor RSX, I'm supprised that no one has
|> mentioned the VAX CoProcessor/RSX package that the good folks at DEC are 
|> selling these days.	
|> 
|>      This package enables a microVAXen to hook up up to 6 KXJ11 CPU's to the
|> q-bus backplane and run RSX.  I'm not sure what role the VAX processor and VMS
|> play in this configuration (maybe an I/O processor :-) :-) :-), but it would
|> seem to me that this evolved out of the 11/74 program.

Not really all that much.  The major motivation was the fact that after the 
first VAXen the PDP-11 compatibility mode was not present in all VAX hardware
models.  The microVAX got a software emulator that proved somewhat less than
overwhelming in performance (I'd still love to benchmark it on the 9000 though!)
and there were enough VAX-11/RSX sites to justify efforts to provide support.
The CoProcessor/RSX package was the result.

CPR didn't really draw a great deal from the 11/74 because the '74 was a
more or less symmetric multiprocessor configuration.  Most importantly, it was
a homogeneous multiprocessor configuration, with all processors executing the
same instruction set and running the same code.

The microVAX handles I/O and file systems, and quite a few other things (logical
name space gets interesting!).  Task installs and loads took a bit of doing too.
All in all, it proved a much bigger effort than was originally anticipated, and
that proved the ultimate undoing of CPR v2 (which was originally an ambitious
plan to replace the microVAX backplane with an ethernet cable).
 
|>      Now, if we could get a 50 MHz version of the J-11 chipset... :-)

The only reason it hasn't happened is that sales forecasts did not justify completion of the project.  I don't know the exact status of the effort, or
the MHz ratings targetted, but a DCJ-11 replacement was investigated a few years
ago.  Do you know that it costs considerably more than pocket change to bring a
new chip to market?

 
|>      Is it just co-incidence, or is there some grand significance to the fact
|> that the 30'th aniversery of the introduction of the PDP-11 will be 2001???

Given the choice between coincidence, or our making schedule over a 30 yr span, 
I'll bet on coincidence!
:-)

--bruce mcculley
 


