In article <720323w.687611404@aucs>, 720323w@aucs.acadiau.ca (Bill Wilder) writes:
> I'll soon be integrating a DEC Station 3100 on a LAN with a VMS host.
> The 3100 will be equipped with DECNet/Ultrix. What would I gain
> in functionality by installing Ultrix Connection on my VMS host?
 
Your VMS host will be capable of acting as NFS server, (not as client).
That will allow you of read and write files located on the VMS machine
from the DECstation as if they where stored on a UNIX machine.
However, Ultric connection (UCX) does not hide the record structure
to the UNIX machines.  So only files in the STREAM_LF format is
presented in the same way on the VMS machine and on the ULTRIX
machine.

DEC has products for submitting jobs from the VMS machine on the
DECstation and for remote procedure calls between the two machines too.

Please note that Wollongong and TGV are selling TCP/IP products with
superior functionallity compared to UCX.  TGVs Multinet is normally
considered the best.  Try sending an E-mail to postmaster@twg.com
and postmaster@tgv.com.

> Also: I've been presuming that stock ULTRIX can provide LAT service
> to my DEC-Server connected terminals. Please let me know if this is
> a naive assumption.
 
 You are right.
