Lines: 64

In the September 23 issue of Digital Review there is a Digital
advertisement, "Digital Update", on page 39 & 40 for the new Turbo
PrintServer 20 laser printer. The last part of the ad is a claim that a
"major U.S. insurance company...[ran]...a huge PostScript job" to test
the Turbo PrintServer 20, and found that it printed the job at 20 pages
per minute. The last line of the ad is "Enough said".

But what I want to know is; Did they buy it?

We have been looking closely at the Turbo PrintServer 20, among other
laser printers, and have been very disappointed with a couple of aspects
of the Turbo PrinterServer 20.

The Turbo PrinterServer 20 appears to have no option to cycle around the
input or output trays. This means on a large print job, > 1000 pages,
the printer will simply stall waiting for either the input tray to be
replenished or the output tray to be cleared. Other laser printers,
notably the very similar QMS PS-2000, have an option where they will
automatically cycle around the available input and output trays, thereby
giving the operator a chance to replenish the paper or clear the output
without pausing the output. This is a fatal flaw for us, wishing to do
print runs or the order of 10000 pages in one job.

The other major gripe is that the Turbo PrintServer 20 requires version
4 of the PrintServer Supporting Host Software. Unfortunately version 4
of the software is an enormous leap backward from the previous version
(3.2). In changing it to support U*ix as well as VMS they have obviously
rewritten it in C, but it doing so they have also radically changed the
user interface as well. Instead of version 3.2 LPS$MANAGEMENT's menus,
there is a new LPS$CONSOLE program with a decidely U*ix flavour. For
example; the Help in LPS$CONSOLE is quite unlike VMS help (the main
entry is through "?") and is not interactive, and LPS$CONSOLE has only a
line-mode interface with U*ix style commands (somewhat cryptic) that
does not following VMS syntax conventions. When the software hit a
problem instead of the customary Facility-Severity-Id convention of VMS
error messages you may receive cryptic U*ix style messages (such as
suggesting you supply the "-N arg or $NODENAME env")! In summary it has
the feel of a cheap-and-nasty conversion of U*ix software done with
scant regard for the VMS environment.

On top of all that they seemed to have introduced a lot of bugs with the
Turbo PrintServer 20/Supporting Host Software combination.

We have contacted several people in Digital Australia to resolve these
questions, but unfortunately this is a very new product for them and
they are floundering at the moment.

My questions;

 . is the "insurance company" quoted in Digital's ad on the net, and are
   they willing to share their experiences?

 . has anyone else any significant experience with the *Turbo*
   PrintServer 20?

 . does anyone else care to recommend an alternative laser printer for
   doing print runs of greater than 10000 pages which can hang off the
   Ethernet (alternative investigated so far are Xerox 4650, QMS PS-2000
   and HP LaserJet IIIsi, none of which are quite suitable-for varying
   reasons)?

