|> MOS memory system was originally supposed to be used.  The MK11 was 
|>retrofitted to the 11/70's later.
|> 
|> The machine had a modified backplane with different cache and memory 
|> management and a board called the IST (some kind of sanity timer which
|> [rumour said] would sound a sonalert on the failed CPU.
|> 
|> The suckers ran at DEC for years.  DEC developed and built their RSX masters 
|> on this one.  I had 1/4 of one at DEC Princeton.  DEC put it there when they
|> needed 11/70's for AT&T and were unable to build any.
|> 
|> It supposedly ran faster than 11/780's.
|> 

We decommissioned CASTOR:: as the RSX build engine in January 1989.  It was a
quad processor PDP-11/74 configuration, but the last couple of years field
service had not been able to keep all four CPUs alive.  Last I heard, at least
part of it had been shipped to somebody in the Colorado Springs CSC and was
running but without service coverage.  

That was probably the world's biggest PDP-11 configuration ever.  I can't find
any notes to refresh my memory, but there were four CPUs, four fully configured
memory boxes (@4mb each), four switched Unibus runs, about 8 DB type disk drives
(mostly RP06s), a couple of RM05s, a few RM03s, a few RK07s, a few RK06s, a few
RAs (mostly 60s and 80s, that was before the current generation), plus a couple
of RK05s, a couple of RL02s, about six tape drives, a CR11, a pair of line
printers, a PC11 (I think, it may've been a PR11), plus a few other odds and 
ends (maybe an ML11, I think).  The config diagram covered a large whiteboard
in small print, and made it obvious at a glance why one possible explanation of
the decision not to ship the product may have been concern over support issues.

For what it's worth, the SYSBUILD for RSX-11M-PLUS was carefully optimized to
keep the CPUs and disk channels (two master source packs, each dual ported on
different bus runs for minimal contention) fully loaded.  The time for a build
was about 18 to 20 hours wall clock, around 14 hours of CPU time, using that
engine.  Moving to a PDP-11/84 with a single master source pack (on an RA80) 
gave us slightly, but not seriously, lower performance, on the order of 24 hours
wall clock and 18 to 20 hours of CPU.  Incidentally, when I was testing the new
PDP-11/94 CPU we found the SYSBUILD performance went back up significantly, but
I don't have the exact numbers.  

So we really didn't give away a lot of speed by moving up to today's technology
but we reduced the footprint a lot, eliminated quite a bit of waste heat, and
really saved on field service costs.  I suspect a lot of the motivation for the
changeover was that our inhouse field service folks weren't able to take the
suspense of waiting for us to ask them to fix it sometime...

--bruce mcculley
(developer of obsolete software)


