	The 11/45/50/55 machines had dedicated procesor slots for
'fastbus' memory. These machines had two unibuses that were normally connected
together with a jumper. The fastbus memory was dual ported, one was a fastbus
path directly to the processor and the other to the Unibus B. Unibus A was
accessibly only by the processor and had bus arbitration. 

	On one of my machines, I seperated the two busses putting an
11/20 on Unibus B. The only memory the 11/20 had was the fasbus memory
on the 11/50. The 11/20 ran high speed data aquisition, did some signal
processing and left the data for the 11/50 to use. The only pitfall was that
with the two busses split, it was not possible for DMA devices on Unibus A
to do DMA cycles into the fastbus memory since the only path was via Unibus B.
Devices on the 11/20/Unibus B could do DMA to the fastbus memory, but the
control register were not accessible from the 11/50.

Confussed?


