Oh, yes.  I remember the PDP-11/74's -- RSX-11M-Plus was developed
specially for these machines.  As a uniprocessor, they were to replace the
PDP-11/70, which had EMI problems (the joke at the mill was that they
interfeared with air navigation), but could be coupled through shared
memory up to 4 units.  

>The suckers ran at DEC for years.  DEC developed and built their RSX masters on
>this one.

That would be Castor and Pollux (or however they were spelled.)
This was a 4-processor machine running two different OS's, RSX-11M and
RSX-11M Plus (or some such...  I don't really remember now.)  They were
in the "raised floor computer room" at the Spitbrook facility (ZK01)
in Nashua, NH.

>It supposedly ran faster than 11/780's.

My understanding was that, with the optional CIS (commercial instruction
set) hardware, the 11/74 outran the original VAX-11/780's running Cobol
programs.  The way I heard it was that the 11/74 was killed to insure
that AT&T and other large customers would buy enough Vaxen to support
the product line in its initial stages, since the 11/74 was fast enough
for most of the customers' applications.

They (the 11/74s) were FAST compared to the 11/70, though.

Today there's the J-11 chip.  This PDP-11 chip has the support hardware
for multiprocessor configurations.  I don't know if anyone uses those
features, though.

Along with "demand paging" PDP-11s (which I have done on an 11/70,
or was that a '74... I don't remember anymore) this gets out of the
relm of folklore and into alchemy.

