This info is a bit dated now, but I have only recently gained ability to post, 
and someone may be interested.....

Some years ago a colleague upgraded his 11/03 to a 11/23, and found that his 
RKV11/RK05 stopped working because the RKV11 only had 16 bit addressing.

When we looked at it we found that....
1. An RKV11D uses the same backplane as the RK11D, and 3 of the 4 cards from 
the RK11D.  The odd one out not surprisingly was the bus interface card, 
unibus or qbus, depending on model.

2. The controller contained all the functions necessary for 18 bit addressing.

3. The qbus interface had 2 spare bus transceiver circuits.

CONCLUSION
All that was required to make the qbus version into an 18 bit addressing 
device was to hook it up.  AND IT WORKED.  Al you need is a soldering iron, 
wire, a scalpel, and the print set to flesh out my lack of detail.

Basically, you need to connect the spare transceiver chips to the qbus, and to
the address register on another card (which required finding some unused
backplane pins).  You need to modify the circuit which detects I/O Page
addressing (move it from 176whatever to 776etc).  I think there was another
small mod which has departed from my grey matter.  I'm way out of touch with
11's now :-( 

It's so simple, one wonders why DEC didn't market it.  I can only assume they 
made a policy decision that RK05's had to die. (They probably haven't died yet 
:-)

