<Various PDP-11/74 memories>

Of course, RSX-11M-Plus still has full support for the 11/74, if you happen
to have one.   :-)
While I have known this for many years, it was brought home forcefully
late last year (1990).

I was converting a customer's device drivers from RSX-11M to RSX-11M-Plus.
They all had mnemonic 2-character names: MB (Map Board -- a lighted display
map of the facility), AY (AYdin displays), CP (Communications Processor),
etc.   Since they had originally all been written by the same programmer,
they were all pretty similar, and no real problem after the first one was
done.

On the appointed test day, I loaded the first driver and issued the CON
(CONfigure) command to bring the device online.   It worked fine.
I loaded the second driver and CONed it online.   It also worked fine.
I loaded the third driver and tried to CON it online.   This failed with
a puzzling error message.

After an hour or so of debugging, it hit me like a brick: the CON program
had a special-case test.   When I told it to bring controller "A" of
device "CP" online, it thought I was trying to bring online CPU "A" of a
multi-processor system.

We changed the device name from CP to QP.


I have some photos of the 11/74 that I took on a visit to the RSX group in
Nashua several years ago.   I had one of the front panel blown up to poster
size for the wall of my office.   I just wish I could get the real thing
for my "museum".

