In article <1991Oct08.222936.11164@convex.com>, datri@convex.com (Anthony A. Datri) writes:
>>DEC used to make something called a ``Unichannel 15''.
>>I understand this was an 11 and 15 connected via Unichannel.
>>I don't specifically know wheather it was an 11/10 (I had an
>>impression of seomthing smaller :-).
> 
> The PDP-15 used an 11/10 for I/O, actually.
> 

Not every PDP-15, just the very last model - a strange beast called a
PDP-15/XVM. (XV as in Roman numerals, M as in Modified, I think). We had
one in house in late 1976 for a while. I still have the brochure on it, and
I quote:

"XVM Unichannel systems provide true dual processing and accomodate a
variety of PDP-11 peripheral processors. Used in this way, low-speed I/O is
transferred to the PDP-11 where it can be inexpensively interfaced and
automatically spooled without the intervention of the XVM computer".

The PDP-11 part was an 11/10 with 8K words of 16bit memory (the XVM had 32k
words of 18bit core).

The XVM we had (and our own older PDP-15) still had no disks, just DECtapes
(1 inch wide pre-addressed magtapes on open drives). These had a habit of
the reel falling off the drive when in use. If this happened then you just
rolled up the tape back onto the reel from where it had spread across the
floor, then typed CTRL/Q on the KSR to continue. The operating system
(KM15) would twitch the tape to read where it was, then calmly continue
processing. It was one of those technologies that you could see at first
sight that it could never work, but in fact they were very reliable.

I could go on at length as to the wizardry of the coding of some parts of
KM15, like the way that directories on DECtapes were written backwards so
that they could be written and checked with the minimum amount of tape
movement...

I still have the console to our 15 sitting by my desk, with some of its
core.

