>>   What's the lowdown on making third party SCSI hard disk drives
>>   work on DECstations?  Is it easy, possible, barely possible?
>>   What kind of software might be required?  Can DEC's software
>>   be tricked into formatting/partitioning/etc non-DEC drives?
>>   -- 
>>   Jurgen Botz                  |   Internet: JBotz@mtholyoke.edu
>>   Academic Systems Consultant  |     Bitnet: JBotz@mhc.bitnet
>>   Mount Holyoke College        |      Voice: (US) 413-538-2375 (daytime)
>>   South Hadley, MA, USA        | Snail Mail: J. Botz, 01075-0629



	It's currently possible and (I hope) it's rapidly approaching
	easy. At a quick overview:

	* Today's Ultrix SCSI driver is completely compatible with a SCSI-2
	  hardware interface. In point of fact, the DS5000/xxx machines all
	  use the NCR53C94 SCSI controller chip currently.

	  If the disk drive you want to use is a SCSI-2 compliant interface
	  then you shouldn't have much trouble at all. If it's a SCSI-1
	  interface but complies with the CCS (Common Command Set) code it
	  should work as well.

	  The majority of the issues we find with the existing interface
	  fall into two categories:

		1. Firmware interaction - Many existing SCSI devices don't
		   pay enough attention to their firmware. In an attempt to
	  	   make this a little easier, we've incorporated some changes
	 	   to a new SCSI PROM for the DS5000/200 systems and for the
		   PMAZ-AA/AB SCSI Host Adapter (the new PROM part number is
		   70-28358-01).

		2. Assumptions that the Ultrix SCSI driver has made about
		   the third party disk. For instance, the Ultrix SCSI
		   driver presumes that all third party disks are asynchronous
		   in operation. A synchronous drive can (if it doesn't do
		   a clean firmware implementation) hang the bus. This can
		   be fixed with a modification of the scsi_data.c file to
		   identify the drive as capable of synchronous mode.


	* The future of the Ultrix SCSI driver is CAM. We are currently in
	  Field Test on the SCSI CAM interface. CAM (Common Access Method)
	  is a proposed IEEE specification that will provide a standard
	  software interface for SCSI in the same fashion that the ANSI SCSI
	  specifications do for the wire protocol. The CAM committee is made
	  up of most of the folks who sit on the ANSI SCSI committee. 

	  This should make life pretty simple in the near future. And that
	  means simple for things other than disks, tapes and CD-ROMs as
	  well.





	One other item we've been working on (recommended by an ISV in
	Massachusetts) is to create table entries for as many types of
	disk drives as we can. Of course, we'll need to include the firmware
	revison on the disk drive as well. If you have entries for your
	favorite drives and can provide the firmware information as well
	I'd be very happy to hear from you. We DO want to make this easy
	for you and we DO want your business.

				Thanx,
