Disclaimer first: these are my personal opinions alone, and do not reflect any
position of Digital Equipment Corporation.

I am only slightly familiar with Mentec.  They are a customer of our 
semiconductor folks who sell them the J-11 chipset.  What they do with it is
entirely their own business.

The claim of compatibility with all PDP software should be a no-brainer.
It's a DEC J-11 chipset running the instruction stream, so it should do that
part just like any other DEC J-11 chipset.  ***Assuming*** that they've got the
system design that incorporates the Jaws chip correct.

I haven't tested the Mentec products, nor do I know anyone in the corporation
who has.  So that is only an assumption about the correctness of their system
design.  Based on my experience with other products, I tend to regard any such
assumptions as unlikely to be entirely true.  The question is to what degree and
in what regard is total correctness of design violated, and does that cause any
visible and objectionable symptoms to the user.  Different users might have
different views on that...

That configuration described does sound a lot like the KDJ11-E board used in the
PDP-11/94 and MicroPDP-11/93.  The E-type (I like the sound of that, wish I had
the one made by Jaguar motorcars!) has 8 serial lines, and the interaction of
memory, CPU, and bus is optimized so that there is not only no-wait-state memory
there is also overlap between bus handshakes and memory cycles.  I spent over a
year testing the KDJ11-E (both before and after introduction) and know that 
there were a couple of problems in that area which have been fixed.  I would 
tend to be very cautious about any board of comparable complexity and perfomance
which I did not know to have had testing as rigorous as I know the KDJ11-E to
have endured, but that's just my opinion.  Your mileage may vary...

I have no knowledge that would allow me to form an opinion about the performance
of the Mentec board.  I do know that the KDJ11-E has a very significant increase
in performance in some applications, and not as great an improvement in others,
because of the different mix of CPU and I/O loads.  One thing I know it has
