In article <1991Oct11.150818.25499@newserve.cc.binghamton.edu>, 
         kym@bingvaxu.cc.binghamton.edu (R. Kym Horsell) writes:

> In article <1991Oct10.173245.1000@lsl.co.uk> paul@lsl.co.uk (Paul Hardy) writes:
>>I could go on at length as to the wizardry of the coding of some parts of
>>KM15, like the way that directories on DECtapes were written backwards so
>>that they could be written and checked with the minimum amount of tape
>>movement...
> 
> Ahhh, the memories. :-) Wasn't it the case that all blocks ended up
> in the right order? I seem to remember the hardware could
> _physically_ write the blocks backward but, if written in the reverse
> direction, wrote them back-to-front...
> 
> -kym

That's right, the code for the DECtape filing system used to reverse the 
order of the data for each block in memory, as well as the sequence of the
blocks, before writing it out with the tape moving backwards.

This was to optimise the common case of creating a file somewhere up the 
tape and then updating the directory which was at the front of the tape.

The reliability of the DECtape drives was legendary, despite their
incredibly simple construction, largely because it wrote each piece of data
four times, twice side by side, and twice along the tape.
You didn't get a lot of data on a tape, though. But as the whole computer
only had 8Kwords of memory, and had the processing speed of a modern
calculator, you weren't writing mega-big programs!

A PDP-15 with four DECtape drives task building a program was
a sight to behold. You tended to have a system tape, a sources tape, a
destination tape, and a library tape, all twitching away at (more or less)
the same time.

Did I read somehere that the new DAT (Digital Audio Tape) drives use
pre-addressed tape, so that it can find and rewrite any given block ?

Maybe we could re-invent tape-based computers !

