It was probably me.

Our site is "pure vax", a small CI VAXcluster consisting of an 8550, an
8530 and an 8200.  There are a couple of MicroVAXes, one in the machine
room and one at the end of a long wire and a microwave line, both
clusters.  Five VAXes.  Let's open a few bonnets....

Two HSCs, an HSC50 and an HSC70, both using pdp11 chips, the F11 (11/23)
and J11 (11/73) respectively.

The 85xx processors have DEC Pro/350s as console processors, both F11
based.

In the comms room lurk three functioning DECSA boxes.  These are all
running Ethernet Terminal Server software, and make up the earliest LAT
boxes (apart from "pure" pdp11 based machines).  These are a pdp11/24
CPU, 512K bytes of memory (on a PMI), a console interface of sorts, a
DEUNA and two PAM (Protocol Assist Modules), each PAM occupying two hex
slots and driving eight two-port line cards each.  The top half of the
box contains a somewhat custom built Unibus backplane (containing CPU,
PAMs etc), the bottom half is two double width linecard planes.

So, we have five VAXes and seven pdp11s.  

Alas, the DECSAs' days are numbered.  They go off maintenance at the end
of the month, to be replaced by six DECserver 300s.  DEC finally admitted
that they really couldn't provide the service on the things that we were
paying an arm and a leg for, after a fourth DECSA developed a flake and
replacing every module and both power supplies in the machine failed to
fix it.  Another DECSA is displaying similar symptoms (crashing
occasionally).  The two fully functional ones will be kept running until
they die.

There are a few of these things around functioning as network routers;
our network DECSA was decommissioned some time ago after X.25 support was
withdrawn; it now lies stripped to the bones in a cupboard after its guts
were used in an attempt to make the dead one go.  Ever seen a pile of
DECSA?

