In <1991Oct10.035516.10464@gorgon.uucp> dag@gorgon.uucp (Daniel A. Glasser) writes:

>They (the 11/74s) were FAST compared to the 11/70, though.

>Today there's the J-11 chip.  This PDP-11 chip has the support hardware
>for multiprocessor configurations.  I don't know if anyone uses those
>features, though.

Well, DEC used the J-11 to make a co-processor board that plugs into a
MicroVAX II backplane, and allows you to run CoProcessor RSX on the J-11.
CoProcessor RSX talks to VMS whenever it needs to talk to a terminal or
disk or other I/O device.  This allows running most user written M+
programs on a MicroVAX II, since the MicroVAX II does not have the PDP-11
compatibility-mode instruction set that was built into some (all?) of the
11/7xx VAXs.

