In article <1991Oct3.051545.24120@tcsc3b2.tcsc.com>, helen@tcsc3b2.tcsc.com (Helen O'Boyle) writes:
> Is there perhaps a monitor subroutine one can invoke to access a low-level
> formatting routine on the controller?  Is there a low-level formatting
> utility on one of the tapes we should have sitting around in the office?

  You should have a "MVII Customer Diagnostics" tape or stack of floppies.
They can re-format a disk that has previously been formatted properly for
a DEC controller. Not very useful. Also, unless you have a recent version
(which you won't have unless you subscribe to the diagnostic updates), it
is probably too out-of-date to work. "MVII Maint. Diagnostics" are licensed
seperately, and will do what you need, provided you have 'em (most sites
don't) and that they are at the current revision level.

  As a further caution, you should make sure that you have an RQDX3 disk
controller (M7555) as opposed to an RQDX1 or 2 (M8639 variants), and that
it is up to rev (the EPROMs on it are 339/340). If you don't have a current
RQDX3, the drive will most likely fail to format.

  As an alternative, it can be formatted with the resident diagnostics in
a VAXstation 2000, or in a PDP-11 with the commonly-available "Micro-11
Formatter" which costs under $25 from DEC.

