A thousand pardons if I've not made myself clear.  Please allow me to
restate what I was saying.

First of all, let me state that I speak only of 3max (DS5000/200), 
pmax (DS3100) and 3min (DS5000/120?) systems.  I know very little of
other DECsystems, and even less of VAXen.   You mentioned workstations
in your posting, and the thread of the discussion is these machines, 
so I ethnocentrically assumed you meant one of these.

I do believe that third-party memory boards for these systems (MBftS) are
fully equivalent to DEC boards.  In order for them to work, they HAVE to 
be.  Furthermore, I am of the capitalist persuasion and believe that it 
is wonderful that you can choose to buy memory from whomever you wish!

What I was trying to say was that DEC MBftS include no self-test.  Claiming
that a MBftS has "all the built-in diagnostics and self-test that DEC's 
has" is like claiming that Brand A radios come with "all the radio 
stations Brand B's receive".  The system board does all the testing.
The memory board just sits there and lets itself be abused.

You should not use that claim to single out Clearpoint as a "better"
vendor, although they certainly may be.  Nor should you bash DEC for
not informing you of that fact, although in general I am all in favour
of informed DECbashing.  What you should do is pick a vendor based on
price, reliability, warranty, or whatever features you care about -- but 
don't be taken in by meaningless claims.

In your later post, you mention some boards (for VAXen) that "support
EDC, on-board diagnostics," etc.  I know nothing of these systems, and
do not (cannot) refute their claim.  Perhaps these machines have smart
memory boards that test themselves -- I've worked on systems like that.
All I'm saying is that the systems mentioned above are not like that.

>Could it be that they're offering error detection and correction that
>DEC doesn't offer?
It isn't likely for the DS5000/200 SIMMs, since they already provide
ECC bits.  It is entirely possible that they've come up with a design
for the DS3100/etc that includes onboard ECC logic that corrects SBE's*
and reports MBE's as parity errors.  It is also possible that they
have done so in such a fashion as to remain competitive pricewise.
Furthermore, it is even possible that they have chosen not to tell
anyone about this miracle nor use it to their competitive advantage.
I haven't seen their boards, so I can't say.  But what do YOU think? :-)

^E

* SBE = single bit error, MBE = multibit error

[ as before, these are my opinions.  I do not speak for DEC. ]
-- 
