In article <kuVg01w164w@apres.UUCP>, system@apres.UUCP (System Operator) writes:
> Does any have a source for [old] documentation concerning
> RA-81 disk drives?  Specifically, I need to know how to
> connect a single drive to a MicroVAX II (in a World Box)
> and how to connect *two* to the MicroVAX.  I *think* I
> have the cabling right for the two drives, but I'm not sure
> about the single drive.  Besides the cable connections,
> do I need a terminator on the SEQ plug?

  The computer (no matter what model) will have a bulkhead with 4 sockets,
with barely legible numbers 0-3. The actual numbers don't matter. The RA81
has 2 attached cables, which terminate in a similar bulkhead on the other
end of the cables from the drive. This bulkhead has labels A and B. Take a
BC26 cable and connect it between the drive cable's bulkhead A connector
and any of the 4 ports on the CPU bulkhead. Make sure the A port button on
the drive is in and the B one is out.

  The cables only go on one way (they're keyed). Also, you can't just take
the bulkhead off the drive cable and plug the drive cable into the CPU bulk-
head - each bulkhead performs a pin swap.

  For a second drive, do the same thing, but pick any of the 3 remaining
empty spots on the CPU bulkhead. Make sure that the two drives have differ-
ent unit select numbers (on the "ready" light). It may also be necessary
that they both be below 16, if you are using a non-HSC controller (which
you are). 0 and 1 are typical. If you have to change the setting, remember
that it's done by breaking off pins on the ready plug, and that you can't
change back.

  In order for the drive to spin up, you need something in the "SEQ IN"
connector on each drive. Normally, the first drive would have a grant plug
there, and it's SEQ out would be cabled to the next drive's SEQ IN. There
is no need to connect anything to the last drive's SEQ OUT. If you can
stand the surge, you can simply put grant plugs in both drives. Don't con-
nect more than 3 drives in a sequence chain, or the controller may time
out waiting for a response from the drive(s) at the end of the chain.

