In article <1991Sep30.164907.20752@pinet.aip.org> marc@pinet.aip.org
(Marc Wiener) writes:
>I get several of these messages every time we 
>reboot (DEC 5000/200 Ultrix 4.1). I haven't been able to find  out what
>it means. Any info would be appreciated.
>
>
>----- EVENT INFORMATION -----
Ultrix errorlog stuff deleted...
>MESSAGE                                 Fixed up unaligned data access for pid
>                                         _191 (iq) at pc 0x402114


Hi.

If this is printing out on your console (screen?), you can set up the
error logger to not print out this kind of message (I think, as it's
been a long time...).

In any event, what caused this is a reference to some data that was not
aligned on a "natural" boundry (e.g. an integer [word] at an odd address
(non-word aligned) address).  Whereas the VAX family of machines
supported this (and you paid for it in performance), typically RISC
machines do not like improperly aligned data.  The Mips architecture,
however, does allow for this by supporting a trap for such events.  The
trap handler (in this case, in Ultrix) simply unwinds the stack to see
what was going on and does the proper fetches and shifting, then resumes
execution.

In short, this message is simply a notification that this occured; the
kernel trap handler did all the right stuff.  However, there is some
program which is executed at boot time that has something
misaligned....

