From: murphy@burfle.dco.dec.com (Rick Murphy)
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
Subject: Re: What does this mean
Message-ID: <1991Oct1.210352.6938@decuac.dec.com>
Date: 1 Oct 91 21:03:52 GMT
References: <1991Sep30.164907.20752@pinet.aip.org>
Sender: news@decuac.dec.com (USENET News System)
Reply-To: murphy@burfle.dco.dec.com
Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation, Landover MD
Lines: 15

That means that you have a program called 'iq' that's accessing data
not on a natural bound. (Like an 'int' straddling a byte address.) One of
the ways RISC systems get their speedups is by not handling misaligned data;
the kernel gets involved, fixes up the attempted access, and allows your
program to continue (if it can.) If these are frequent, you're taking a large
performance hit (not to mention eating disk space for the error log.)

You'll have to find out what 'iq' is and fix whatever's causing the
unaligned data access.
	-Rick
