Subject: Third party drives running synchronous...
Message-ID: <AJC.91Oct3215943@thendara.pa.dec.com>
Date: 3 Oct 91 20:59:43 GMT
Sender: news@PA.dec.com (News)
Distribution: comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.ultrix
Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
Lines: 87



	Folks,

	  I remember somebody posting a note not too long ago explaining
	how to get a Fujitsu drive running on a DECstation. If I recall
	correctly (and Young Master Gettys will still tell you I'm going
	senile) the suggestion was to modify the jumper setting so that
	the drive would not attempt to negotiate synchronous mode transfers.

	  Not being the brightest person to come down the pike in a while,
	it took me some time to figure out what might be going on. I don't
	believe that you have to give up the increased performance of using
	synchronous transfers in order to get the drive to run.

	  Contrary to the persons post, DEC does support synchronous mode
	in Ultrix. And has for quite some time. The last disk drive we had
	that didn't support synchronous was the RZ23. Not only do all of
	our other disk drives support synchronous, they will even attempt
	to negotiate synchronous transfer themselves.

	  Here's what should happen with a DEC drive:

	* The host sends a request to the target (DEC drive) requesting
	  a transfer, but without negotiating synchronous mode.

	* The DEC drive will respond in aysnchronous mode.

	* The next time the host attempts a request to the DEC drive, the
	  DEC drive will attempt a synchronous response (NOTE: An improperly
	  implemented SCSI 1 host may hang at this point). If the response
	  is acknoledged as a successful transfer, the drive will stay in
	  synchronous mode. If the response is not acknoledged as successful,
	  the disk drive should drop back to asynchronous mode and stay there.


	  Here's what should happen with a Third Party Drive on a DECstation:

	* Ultrix believes that all third party drives are asynchronous only
	  (lore has it that this was done because of the number of complaints
	  about SCSI 1 drives that hung when the system attempted synchronous
	  negotiation).

	* The host sends a request to the target (third party drive) without
	  attempting to negotiate synchronous transfer.

	* The target responds in asynchronous mode.

	* The next request that the host makes of the target, the target may
	  attempt to negotiate a synchronous transfer.

	* Because the scsi_data.c table tells the DECstation that this drive
	  can't run synchronous, the response will not be acknoledged as a
	  successful synchronous transfer.

	* The target, upon not receiving such an acknoledge, should complete
	  the response in asynchronous mode. However, some disk drives which
	  don't quite hit the right implementation of firmware, may hang in
	  a wait state expecting that at sometime the host will provide an
	  acknowledge of a successful transfer.


	  What you can do about your third party drive:

	If you buy a drive (say like Fujitsu) which you believe supports the
	synchronous mode transfer, you can go into the scsi_data.c file and
	modify the entry for third party disks to indicate synchronous. If
	the drive is properly implemented, you should have no problems in
	running the drive synchronous from there on out.


	I hope that this helps. If you do find drives where this doesn't seem
	to help, TRI/ADD would be glad to talk to your disk drive vendor about
	it. Just have them give us a call.

				Thanx,
				  AJ


